A||N||57||Will||Turbo GP cylinder heads vs 3400 SFI heads||||1||
Z||000000||Will||07-07-2001||03:41 PM||||A local junkyard just got a Turbo 3.1 Grand Prix.<P>If there is any chance at all I can use that engine's exhaust manifolding on an 3400 SFI I will do it.<P>Is there such a chance?<P>What heads are on the 3100 turbo? Gen II?<P>How do those exhaust ports compare to the Gen III heads on the 3400 SFI?<P>If I can't put the turbo parafenalia on a 3400, I might as well just drop the whole 3100 into that '87 GT 5 spd I have lying around...||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||07-07-2001||06:33 PM||||.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Will||07-07-2001||07:00 PM||||I believe there's no replacement for displacement. 3.4 litre at 15 psi will make more power than 3.1 litre at 15 psi, not to mention that the 3.4 has better cylinder heads.<P>Idealism aside, just dropping the 3100 turbo into my Fiero is likely to be the best bet. And if it gets that heavy, no traction front wheel drive GP into the 13's, then it should push a Fiero pretty well.<P>What ECM issues will arise from the manual transmission?<BR>Will I need any "tuning" changes to crank the boost up a few psi?<BR>What was stock boost?<BR>It will be intercooled. I'm going to find an AC evaporator from something large and use it as an intercooler. I'm going to add an extra condenser to the Fiero A/C system and essentially air condition the inlet air.<BR>Thanks for the offer of help! I'll definitely need it.<P>Has anyone done this swap before?<BR>What did they do about accessory mounting?<BR>The only problem seems to be the alternator, which want to be in the same place as the body side Fiero dogbone bracket.<P>Oh yeah, do those engines really have forged cranks?<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 07-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||hugh||07-08-2001||11:19 AM||||An evaporator will not work as an intercooler.I assume you are meaning to box it to pull air through the coil over the fins.Basically in a straight line run(drag race)the mass of the intercooler does the cooling since the duration of the race is relatively short.The best type of inter cooler if you are limited for space in the engine compartment is a water to air intercooler,but they require more space elsewhere,as well as plumbing,a water pump,and another radiator to reject the heat developed by the compressed inlet air.A water to air cooler transfers heat 9 times faster than air to air. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||07-08-2001||04:56 PM||||I've had enough of people telling me that a freon intercooler won't work without giving a good reason why.<BR>Either stop telling me it won't work or show me where my thermodynamic calculations are wrong.<P>Back to the original topic:<P>Does the 3100 turbo wear Gen II heads or Gen III?<BR>If it wears Gen II heads, which I am assuming it does, how does the exhaust port shape and spacing compare to the Gen III heads on a modern 3400 SFI?<BR>Will the turbo exhaust manifolds bolt up to the Gen III heads?||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||07-08-2001||06:18 PM||||Of course a freon intercooler will work. It's done by several companies.||||reg||1
Z||000006||hugh||07-08-2001||06:31 PM||||I'll tell you what.When I try to help and get that kind of response for my effort,you better believe you won't be hearing from me again!||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||07-08-2001||07:23 PM||||I think I hear my mom calling me..........||||reg||5
Z||000008||hugh||07-08-2001||07:49 PM||||Terryk,After you answer your mom maybe you will give me some information on freon intercoolers.I'm not too old to learn something new.If it's out there I would like to learn what I can about it.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||07-08-2001||09:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by hugh:<BR><B>I'll tell you what.When I try to help and get that kind of response for my effort,you better believe you won't be hearing from me again!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm sorry you feel that way, but we've had this discussion once before several months ago: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20010418-2-005666.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20010418-2-005666.html</A> <P>It looks as though neither of our opinions has changed; it went about the same way then, too.<P>If you want to do the calculations, break out your college physics book and review the ideal gas law, obtain figures for the spcific heat and density of air, mass flow per horsepower, and make an educated guess as to compressor efficiency.<P>I have an inexpensive reference volume which has all the data needed. I can tell you where to get one if you'd like.<P>Anyway, back when the thread mentioned above was in progress, I did some calculations:<P>To cool 600 HP worth of air from between 300 and 350 degrees F (ridiculously hot) to about 40 degrees F would take six times the capacity of the stock Fiero AC system.<P>Cooling 300 HP worth of air from a more reasonable 250 degrees to a more reasonable 70 degrees would take about twice the capacity of the stock Fiero system. Add an evaporator/intercooler in back, plumb in an extra condenser in front, work some control magic, et voila: we have a freon intercooler.<P>Please don't tell me it won't work, because I've run the numbers. Even if the numbers are wrong I'm going to try it and see.<P>I appreciate the fact that you have a fast turbocharged car and know a fair amount about intercooling. I would appreciate any constructive input you may have, but please don't don't tell me it won't work; that's not constructive.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||hugh||07-08-2001||10:10 PM||||Will,I shouldn't have been so negative.I wish you good luck in your project.Keep us informed of your progress.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Terrybogin||07-08-2001||10:37 PM||||Interesting- But won't the drag of the compressor be about the same gain made by freon cooling vs. air to air, or water to air? Will an A/C compressor survive when it is turned up 6,000+ rpm's? I am curious how this will turn out. Please keep us old timers updated.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Will||07-08-2001||11:34 PM||||I <I>hope</I> the compressor survives  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I won't be exceeding the factory redline, which is 6K according to <A HREF="http://www.gm.com/automotive/gmpowertrain/engines_cartruck/other/34_main.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gm.com/automotive/gmpowertrain/engines_cartruck/other/34_main.htm</A> <P>I don't think parasitic loss will be an issue<BR> 300 - 10 HP is still greater than 0 <BR>(since the engine will blow itself up and output ZERO horse power if the intake charge gets too hot and it detonates under boost)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>IOW, better this than nothing.<P>I'm ordering a 3400 SFI exhaust manifold gasket and a 3100 Turbo exhaust manifold gasket from my local GM parts supplier tomorrow. I'll compare them and see if what I want to do is possible.<P>Do the 3100 Turbo engines really have forged cranks?<BR>A forged crank would do well in a 3400 TDC spinning to 8500 RPM  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||Phantom Rage||07-09-2001||12:14 AM||||I hope this sheds some light on the refrigerated intercooling Idea.<P>Yes it has been done, check out this article for more details <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR> <A HREF="http://www.autospeed.com/A_0436/page1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.autospeed.com/A_0436/page1.html</A> <BR>||||reg||7
Z||000014||Formula 18||07-09-2001||11:12 AM||||Water Injection is the way to go IMO. It is very efficent and fairly easy to plumb in. You could probally do it for 150 dollars if you wanted to put together your own kit. <P>It works by spraying a mist of water into the compressed air and reduces the heat by boiling. (Look at the old physic books--&gt; heat needed to boil water is much great than heat needed to rais it's temp) <P>I am sure there are post about it here if you run a search.<P><BR>Peace <BR>Bri||||reg||1
Z||000015||ray b||07-09-2001||01:26 PM||||water+alcohol works better i knew a guy who was hooked up with the man [Wang]that did the work on the ww2 fighters. they made car kits in the 70's gas crisis to run super lean but run out of h20+al and motor will melt quick.<P>freon intercool will cool some like at drags but not enuff to work all the time on a street car.<P>maybe if you could use another turbo hooked up to compress the freon at a high rate with low drag but thats a lot of work.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd<p>[This message has been edited by ray b (edited 07-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Joe Torma||07-09-2001||07:25 PM||||The Gen III heads that I have on my 3400 series conversion have D shaped exhaaust ports and the Gen II have Round.  The Gen III valves are like 15% bigger.<P><BR>Joe<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Will||07-09-2001||08:20 PM||||Thank you.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Will||07-15-2001||10:33 PM||||I looked at axhaust manifold gaskets from a '90 3100 and a '98 (?) 3400.<BR>The exhaust manifold bolt holes are the SAME and the port roofs are the SAME.<BR>The 3100 gasket has D-shaped holes, while the 3400 gasket has round holes.<BR>The bottom centers of the holes line up however, so a little gasket matching will make everything hunky-dory.<P>3400 Turbo, here I come!||||reg||1
Z||000019||Joe Torma||07-16-2001||07:02 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>.<BR>The 3100 gasket has D-shaped holes, while the 3400 gasket has round holes.<BR>3400 Turbo, here I come![/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Don't you mean the 3400 had the D shape?  I haven't seen the 3100 series...maybe they are using the Ds also.  The gaskets are interesting aren't they?  Metal expanding construction?!<P>So you are going with the 3400?<BR>Very nice setup.  "4 Bolt"(kinda) mains even.<BR>What are you going to do with the alternator?<P>Scoops are good.<BR>Joe<P><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 07-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||00lE||07-16-2001||10:11 PM||||GM started using the 3100 series SFI motor at the end of 1994, so a 1990 3100 does not even exist.  Perhaps you meant the 90 3.1 L MPFI?<BR>     Both the 3100 and 3400 use the same GEN III aluminum cylinder heads with D-shaped exhaust ports.  The 3.1 MPFI engines used the older GEN II heads with round exhaust ports.  <BR>     The more advanced GEN III heads together with an improved engine managment system allows the 3100/3400's to run at a 9.6:1 compression ratio on regular pump gas.  This comp. ratio is still right at the edge of the detonation threshold for these engines, but the newer PCM's employ a knock sensor to compensate (within reason) for this by retarding the timing (a max of ~30 degrees) and thus preventing potential detonation.  Just something to keep in mind if you intend on further increasing the pressure in these combustion chambers without changing to a lower compression piston (eg. big boom).<P>     If you are really set on turbocharging a 3400 SFI engine, I would suggest just swapping to a set of Fiero iron heads.  The resulting compression ratio would be right around 7.9:1 using the OEM 3400 SFI pistons. I would also suggest shaving the heads a bit and using the thinnest head gasket available.  Otherwise, you would need custom pistons since there are no OEM (3.4 or 3400) pistons available that would drop the compression ratio down appropriatly.  <BR>     The 3100 is a different story, since you can simply swap in a set of stock 3.1 MPFI aluminum head pistons to obtain a ratio of about 8.8:1.<P>Note:  There are some other minor modifications needed to use the iron heads on the 3400 SFI. See :<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/calgaryfieros/3400engine.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/calgaryfieros/3400engine.htm</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 07-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||Joe Torma||07-16-2001||10:58 PM||||Good info...thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Will||07-17-2001||01:57 PM||||OK, maybe what I posted was wrong. I'm relaying what the parts guy told me. Maybe he got his boxes confused, because the one with the D shaped holes was also metal sandwhich construction. Hmmm....<P>At least the bolt patterns are the same.||||reg||1
Z||000023||00lE||07-17-2001||07:46 PM||||The bolt patterns for the 3.1 and the 3400 manifolds should be pretty close.  It would be a very good thing indeed if you could mount 3.1 exhaust manifolds on the 3400 heads without impeding the improved exhaust flow obtaind from the D-shaped port, keep us posted!<p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 07-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||Joe Torma||07-17-2001||09:17 PM||||I made myself some hedders.  Kinda' a pain, but a helluva lot better than the 3.4 iron stockers.<P>Joe <p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 07-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||Will||07-17-2001||11:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 00lE:<BR><B>The bolt patterns for the 3.1 and the 3400 manifolds should be pretty close.  It would be a very good thing indeed if you could mount 3.1 exhaust manifolds on the 3400 heads without impeding the improved exhaust flow obtaind from the D-shaped port, keep us posted!<P>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 07-17-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The bolt pattern is exactly the same.<BR>The top, sides and bottom center of the ports are the same. The only difference is the corners of the "D". <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000026||Will||07-17-2001||11:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 00lE:<BR><B><BR>     If you are really set on turbocharging a 3400 SFI engine, I would suggest just swapping to a set of Fiero iron heads.  The resulting compression ratio would be right around 7.9:1 using the OEM 3400 SFI pistons. I would also suggest shaving the heads a bit and using the thinnest head gasket available.  Otherwise, you would need custom pistons since there are no OEM (3.4 or 3400) pistons available that would drop the compression ratio down appropriatly.  <BR>     The 3100 is a different story, since you can simply swap in a set of stock 3.1 MPFI aluminum head pistons to obtain a ratio of about 8.8:1.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>There are two reasons I want to use a 3400:<BR>1) no replacement for displacement<BR>2) The Gen III heads!<BR>The 3400 N/A makes within 10 HP of what the 3.1 turbo makes because the heads flow like crazy. Now stack forced induction on top of flow like that, factor in the extra displacement, and you don't have to run as much boost to make the same power as a 3.1 Turbo.<BR>What fuel is recommended for the 3400? if it's 87 octane, then that's fine: I'll have 6 points of octane to play with without having to go to special fuel or different components. I can't really see a modern engine with only 9.6 compression requiring anything higher than 87 octane.||||reg||1
Z||000027||Joe Torma||07-18-2001||01:02 AM||||That is pretty much why I went with the 3400.<P>Lets see...<BR>3400 series is at least 80lbs lighter then the 2.8.  Plenty ligher than the 3800 and far less than a V8...and DesktopDyno2000(very accurate to known engines I've compared it to) says for what I've done to it...300hp.<BR>It even takes up less room in the engine compartment it is all roller(except for the rocker's tip) too.<P>I do however think that it it more involved than the other engine swaps, basically because I haven't found anyone who has done it yet.  <P>Maybe I should produce a kit?<P>Joe<P><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 07-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000028||00lE||07-18-2001||05:07 AM||||Other than the displacement, the 3100 is identical to the 3400.  I run a 96' 3100 with Gen III heads in my 88 formula.  Please explain what you did to get 300 hp out of a 3400 without a turbo??||||reg||1
Z||000029||00lE||07-18-2001||06:02 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> The 3400 N/A makes within 10 HP of what the 3.1 turbo makes because the heads flow like crazy.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>According to gmpowertrain.com, the 3400 SFI is rated at 180hp in the W-body, The TGP's turbo 3.1 MPFI is rated at 225hp (thats a 45 hp difference). <BR>If the PCM is using up to 30 degrees of  retard just to inhibit the knock from regular fuel, it is unlikely that you will be able to use more than about 3 - 4 lbs of boost running premium fuel.  According to my calculations, 4 lbs of boost would only produce an extra 30 - 35 hp if you are lucky, and IMO that would not even be worth the trouble...  <BR>     It might be a good idea to at least find a running 3400, and check what I am saying with a scan tool before making a sizeable investment into such a venture.<BR>     If custom pistons really are out of the question, you could use a thicker head gasket instead. Copper head gaskets can be made for just about any application with the extra thickness needed to drop the compression ratio down a bit:  <A HREF="http://www.headgasket.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.headgasket.com/</A><P><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 07-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000030||Will||07-18-2001||03:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 00lE:<BR><B>  According to gmpowertrain.com, the 3400 SFI is rated at 180hp in the W-body, The TGP's turbo 3.1 MPFI is rated at 225hp (thats a 45 hp difference). <P>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 07-18-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Woops. I thought that the 3400 was 185 HP and the 3100 Turbo was 195 HP. Oh Well...<P>How many different flavors of pistons exist for the 3.4/3400?<P>F-body 3.4 pistons (flat top?)<BR>3400 pistons  (dished?)<BR>3400 TDC pistons  (domed with 4 valve reliefs?)<P>What are the various combustion chamber CC's?<BR>Gen I Iron heads<BR>Gen II Aluminum<BR>Gen III Aluminum<P>WTF? GM Powertrain website doesn't have bore & stroke info anymore.||||reg||1
Z||000031||Joe Torma||07-18-2001||05:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>How many different flavors of pistons exist for the 3.4/3400?<P><BR>3400 pistons  (dished?)<BR>What are the various combustion chamber CC's?<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Dished, dished and dished.  The 3400 has 9.5:1 compression...the dish(similar to 3.1 stroker) piston makes some room for the valves.<P>Joe<P>||||reg||1
Z||000032||00lE||07-18-2001||05:15 PM||||     The Gen II and Gen III cyl head volumes are pretty much equal at about 28cc.  The iron heads are around 51cc.<BR>     The problem is there was never a lower compression version of an aluminum head 3.4 produced.  So there are no "off the shelf" pistons available to drop the compression ratio down if you stay with the Gen III head on the 3400.  The 3100 SFI is a much better candidate for boost applications, since you can use a set of the stock (or aftermarket)3.1 MPFI pistons to achieve a compression ratio of around 8.9:1.<P>The 3400 SFI has a bore of 3.622" and a stroke of 3.3096".  The stock pistons have a 3.15" diameter dish that is .260" deep. Compressed height is 1.470" and the Pin diameter is .9055" (Offset).<P>The pistons for the 3.4 iron head or 3.4 DOHC would result in very large increase in compression ratio when used with the Gen III heads, which is exactly what you do not want for a boost application.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000033||Will||07-19-2001||09:00 AM||||Hmmm....<P>I wouldn't think that the PCM normally uses any knock retard at all on regular fuel, just that it has the capacity to do so under extreme conditions like driving through death valley in the heat of the noon-day sun.<P>Good info. I'll keep thinking about this project.||||reg||1
Z||000034||00lE||07-19-2001||10:15 AM||||Well, I am kinda at a slight advantage here.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I have a friend who is a GM service tech.  We were discussing the possiblity of turbocharging my 3100 when this issue came up.  I did not believe him and argued the point.  He took me in the bays and showed me on two differrent brand new Grand AM's (3400 SFI) that were in the shop.  The knock counts are stable at idle, but as soon as any increase in throttle occurs they start to count off like a stopwatch with an accompanied increase in knock retard.  He also said the 3100's are the same way, so I decided to save a bit more money for some internal work before I attempted my turbo installation.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I already had the turbo and most everything else I needed for the conversion, so you can imagine I was a bit disappointed.  In retrospect, I think it is a pretty good idea to install the NA version of the engine and get all the bugs out of it before installing a turbo system, its just a hell of a lot more work that way...||||reg||1
Z||000035||Joe Torma||07-19-2001||11:06 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 00lE:<BR><B>Other than the displacement, the 3100 is identical to the 3400.  I run a 96' 3100 with Gen III heads in my 88 formula.  Please explain what you did to get 300 hp out of a 3400 without a turbo??</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Um, I missed this question...Well, I wouldn't want to give away my secrets now.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Just your normal mods...of course more difficult because nobody has modified this engine that I know of...and no performance parts are available.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dyno.gif"> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000036||00lE||07-19-2001||03:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B> Just your normal mods...of course more difficult because nobody has modified this engine that I know of...and no performance parts are available.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So you claim this unbelievable amount of horsepower, from a motor that we already know has pretty much zilch for aftermarket performance parts available, and yet you want to keep the details secret??  I think your full of $#it! But thats just my opinion..<P>||||reg||1
Z||000037||Formula88||07-19-2001||03:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> If you want to do the calculations, break out your college physics book and review the ideal gas law, obtain figures for the spcific heat and density of air, mass flow per horsepower, and make an educated guess as to compressor efficiency.<P>Please don't tell me it won't work, because I've run the numbers. Even if the numbers are wrong I'm going to try it and see.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, obviously, the proof is in the testing, but I don't think it'll work, and I'll tell you why.  You can run your A/C to cool the evaporator and thus, cool the air coming through it.  That's fine.  Now, how much HP is it going to take to run the A/C?  How is the A/C compressor going to handle the high rpm's you want to run with this engine?  GM either didn't offer A/C on high rpm engines, or they put cutouts on it to kill the A/C under full throttle.  The compressors don't handle the high rpms well.  The only way I can see this working is if you find some way to chill a medium that will keep the evaporator cold for a time after you turn off the A/C.  That way you chill the intercooler in the staging lanes, turn it off when you stage and then run your race.  In effect making a real big cool can.  You could do the same thing with ice. Don't forget, though, that all an A/C does is move heat from one place to another - so in addition to your extra heat from all this extra horsepower, the heat from turbo added to the already hot Fiero engine bay - you're also adding the heat from the condenser back there.  You can't cool anything if you can't expel the heat efficiently.<P>I won't say it CAN'T be done.  But I don't think it'll work like you envision.  Even if it does work, I think you'll get minimal results at best, and certainly not worth the effort.  However, it would be worth it simply from the engineering exercise point.  So build your prototype and let us know if it works.   Good luck.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>BTW, if needbe, I can break out my Mark's Standard Guide for the Mechanical Engineer I got when I was working as an Mechanical Engineer co-op at NASA, or I could get my old refrigeration texts that I used when I did HVAC work.  Let me know if you have any questions. <P>Side note: any ME's out there - if you don't have it, Mark's Std. Guide is an AWESOME reference!!  No ME should be without one.  It's the single most useful reference text I've ever had for engineering work.||||reg||4
Z||000038||Will||07-19-2001||05:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B> Well, obviously, the proof is in the testing, but I don't think it'll work, and I'll tell you why.  You can run your A/C to cool the evaporator and thus, cool the air coming through it.  That's fine.  Now, how much HP is it going to take to run the A/C?  How is the A/C compressor going to handle the high rpm's you want to run with this engine?  GM either didn't offer A/C on high rpm engines, or they put cutouts on it to kill the A/C under full throttle.  The compressors don't handle the high rpms well.  The only way I can see this working is if you find some way to chill a medium that will keep the evaporator cold for a time after you turn off the A/C.  That way you chill the intercooler in the staging lanes, turn it off when you stage and then run your race.  In effect making a real big cool can.  You could do the same thing with ice. Don't forget, though, that all an A/C does is move heat from one place to another - so in addition to your extra heat from all this extra horsepower, the heat from turbo added to the already hot Fiero engine bay - you're also adding the heat from the condenser back there.  You can't cool anything if you can't expel the heat efficiently.<P>I won't say it CAN'T be done.  But I don't think it'll work like you envision.  Even if it does work, I think you'll get minimal results at best, and certainly not worth the effort.  However, it would be worth it simply from the engineering exercise point.  So build your prototype and let us know if it works.   Good luck.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>BTW, if needbe, I can break out my Mark's Standard Guide for the Mechanical Engineer I got when I was working as an Mechanical Engineer co-op at NASA, or I could get my old refrigeration texts that I used when I did HVAC work.  Let me know if you have any questions. <P>Side note: any ME's out there - if you don't have it, Mark's Std. Guide is an AWESOME reference!!  No ME should be without one.  It's the single most useful reference text I've ever had for engineering work.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I haven't run numbers yet, but I would be very surprised if the compressor used up enough power to offset the increased boost and increased charge density. We're talking about a good bit of power here.<BR>The condenser won't be in the engine compartment. One of the beauties of using Freon is that the equipment necessary to move it around the car is already in place. I'd use another evaporator in parallel with the existing one in the <I>front</I> compartment, along with hood louvres and a 240W fan motor to sink heat to the atmosphere.<BR>I'm aware of the possiblity of using an intermediate heat exchange medium and am considering it. In one of the previous intercooler threads there was a link posted to an article about Ford(?)research into a similar concept.<BR>I'm still looking at the concept. I may end up using a water-air intercooler or possibly an air-air unit under one of the deck louvres with one of the aforementioned 240W fan motors mounted between the IC and louvre pulling air up through the IC. I don't know yet, but I am quite interested in a freon intercooler.||||reg||1
Z||000039||Will||07-19-2001||05:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>BTW, if needbe, I can break out my Mark's Standard Guide for the Mechanical Engineer I got when I was working as an Mechanical Engineer co-op at NASA, or I could get my old refrigeration texts that I used when I did HVAC work.  Let me know if you have any questions. <P>Side note: any ME's out there - if you don't have it, Mark's Std. Guide is an AWESOME reference!!  No ME should be without one.  It's the single most useful reference text I've ever had for engineering work.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'll add Mark's Std Guide to my professional library.<BR>What do you have in your HVAC literature about control systems? That's where I'm fuzziest right now.||||reg||1
Z||000040||00lE||07-19-2001||06:30 PM||||Hate to break the bubble on this new and ingenius invention, but I am afraid that it has already been done, marketed, and printed on the t-shirt...<BR> <A HREF="http://www.coolflow.com/prod1.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.coolflow.com/prod1.htm</A> <p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 07-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000041||Joe Torma||07-19-2001||09:47 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 00lE:<BR><B> So you claim this unbelievable amount of horsepower, from a motor that we already know has pretty much zilch for aftermarket performance parts available, and yet you want to keep the details secret??  I think your full of $#it! But thats just my opinion..<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ouch...I wasn't being arrogant or anything.<P>I just entered all the available info for the HO GM Performance 3.4 crate motor(from the website) and Mr Desktop Dyno is (+)4hp off(correct rpm) of the GM specs, The DDyno is a little more off in torque (-)10 ftlb at a little higher rpm.<P>Yes there are no specific aftermarket parts.  I did a lot of homework and research about this engine and parts...it was 1 1/2years in the building.  I wish I could confirm the stock specs on the DDyno, but I don't have specs for the stock cam.<P>So what is in it?  Seriously, all the things you'd do to any other engine.  Port, polish, hedders, etc., etc.<P>If you want part numbers...there aren't any!<P>What kind of engine do you have in your car?  What kind of goodies?<P>Joe<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/3400.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 07-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000042||Will||07-20-2001||08:57 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B> I wish I could confirm the stock specs on the DDyno, but I don't have specs for the stock cam.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Take it to a chassis dyno! 1FST2M6 can tell you where to find one.||||reg||1
Z||000043||Joe Torma||07-20-2001||10:05 AM||||As soon as I finish my car, I'd love to take it to the dyno!  Any in Jersey?<P>I've only driven it twice since I put the 3400 in.  On my way to the Fiero Festival I'll be stopping at the place that makes the new fuel/engine control system...see if they can tweak if for me!<P>I'm also hoping to make it to Maple Grove for Pontiac day.  There is a problem...I'll have 200lbs worth of stereo in the stinking car.  Oh well.<P>Joe||||reg||1
Z||000044||Will||07-20-2001||10:19 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B>As soon as I finish my car, I'd love to take it to the dyno!  Any in Jersey?<P>I've only driven it twice since I put the 3400 in.  On my way to the Fiero Festival I'll be stopping at the place that makes the new fuel/engine control system...see if they can tweak if for me!<P>I'm also hoping to make it to Maple Grove for Pontiac day.  There is a problem...I'll have 200lbs worth of stereo in the stinking car.  Oh well.<P>Joe</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm sure there are plenty of chassis dynos in Joisey.<BR>What kind of engine management system are you using? How much was it?<P>How one earth do you fit 200 lbs of stereo equipment in a Fiero?<P>Did the turbo 3.1's really have forged cranks, as I've heard?||||reg||1
Z||000045||00lE||07-20-2001||03:08 PM||||I did not realize that the HP you stated was an estimate, sorry if I acted a bit caustic.  Nice job on the headers, looks like you have spent considerable time on that powerplant.||||reg||1
Z||000046||00lE||07-20-2001||03:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> <BR>Did the turbo 3.1's really have forged cranks, as I've heard?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The TGP folks claim that the crank in the 3.1 turbo was "hardened", but not forged.<BR>I believe that this is just a big myth, since the crank for the NA version of this engine costs about the same brand new from GM, and GM is not known to go out of production line for something like having a stock crank hardened (ie..it would be avail that way off the shelf).  Here are the part numbers for the cranks, you can verify yourself on gmpartdirect.com :<P>10110549	CRANKSHAFT - LG5, Turbo<P>10078981	CRANKSHAFT - LHO Non-turbo<P>||||reg||1
Z||000047||Joe Torma||07-20-2001||03:39 PM||||OOlE..you do know your stuff!<BR>Here are some pictures of my headers.  They took me about 5 days.  2 to figure out how to join the stupid pipes toghether!<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/exhaust.gif">  <P>Yes the 300hp is an estimate, but if it like all the other confirmed engines...pretty accurate.<P>AS far as 200lbs of stereo...uh, not happy about it, but I'm going to compete in the last few IASCA SoundQuality and dB competetions.<BR>Let me add the junk up...<BR>Audiobahn subs+boxes:  80lbs<BR>Pioneer subs+box 60lbs<BR>3 pioneer amps+1 audiobahn(total 3000+watts RMS):  45lbs<BR>And the amprack and wiring...about 200lbs!<P>I use an Electromotive TEC-II.  <P>The 3400 engine is what, 80-100lbs lighter?  Aluminum heads and many parts, compact starter, not much garbage on the front of the engine.<P>Hey I need the stereo...my exhaust is really loud!!!  Not much for mufflers!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 07-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000048||00lE||07-20-2001||06:56 PM||||If those headers adapt correctly to the D-shaped exhaust ports, I am one envious SOB!<BR>Even if they don't, they are one of most impressive DIY sets I have ever seen on a Fiero..<BR>You must have alot of $$$ invested in that setup, the TEC-II alone is out of my budget.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000049||Joe Torma||07-20-2001||09:06 PM||||Thanks for the compliment!<BR>Well, I'll admit that the pipes are round not D shaped, but the inside diameter of the pipe is matched to the port size...I figured it was good enough.  They are pretty close to being equal length and stepped once to a larger pipe before the collector(I read it prevents reversion and draws the exaust out).<P>The Tec II is $1600.  Expensive, but a lot less trouble than Steve Normington seems to be having!<P>The engine had 6000mi on it, but I replaced the bearings and most everything except for pistons. Got it for $1000.  Parts were expensive.<P><BR>Joe||||reg||1
Z||000050||Will||07-21-2001||12:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B>I use an Electromotive TEC-II.  <P>The 3400 engine is what, 80-100lbs lighter?  Aluminum heads and many parts, compact starter, not much garbage on the front of the engine.<P>Hey I need the stereo...my exhaust is really loud!!!  Not much for mufflers!<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Nice headers!<P>An aluminum head/lightweight intake SBC is about 80 lbs lighter than an iron head iron intake version, but I don't think the weight savings would be that large on an engine as comparatively small as a 60*6. I'd guess 40-50 lbs unless you've weighed everything and can tell me I'm full of S***  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Does the TEC II use the stock GM CKS or does it have its own sensor?||||reg||1
Z||000051||Joe Torma||07-21-2001||02:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> Nice headers!<P>I'd guess 40-50 lbs unless you've weighed everything and can tell me I'm full of S***   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Does the TEC II use the stock GM CKS or does it have its own sensor?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks!<P>The Tec II uses its own sensor bolted to the damper.  I made a bracket for the pickup off of the A/C compressor(since there was a space after deleting some cast iron crappy mount) and it is adjustable because you have to align it with TDC #1 on the 11th tooth.<P>As for the weight savings...should have weighed it!  All I know is any person can pick up the Aluminum head with two fingers! Much, much lighter!<P>Joe<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000052||Will||07-21-2001||09:57 PM||||Joe, how did you deal with the alternator and dogbone bracket wanting to be in the same place?||||reg||1
Z||000053||Joe Torma||07-21-2001||10:22 PM||||Well, I got rid of the original dogbone location.  I was surprised that there is about 3 inches between the alternator and firewall. I will probably be adding another "dogbone", but for now, I have an adjustable "stick" taking the torque and movement.  It runs from the side of the block and goes down to the cradle(above the exhaust).<P>Unfortunately, the alternator pokes out of the lid...so I made a scoop.  It will help with the heat also.<P>Joe||||reg||1
Z||000054||Patrick's Dad||07-21-2001||10:29 PM||||RE: The bottom end:<P>I've been looking up stuff to create a parts list for my upcoming build. First of all, are the journals of the 3.1/3.4 crank the same size as a SBC crank? If so, is there any reason (other than buying two extras) that a/m connecting rods couldn't be used? Both are 5.7" long (normally).<P>2d, Silv-o-lite shows two slightly different pistons for the 3.4 (not TDC). The one they describe as "Camaro" has a 2.75" diameter dish, .080" deep. The other, "Lumina APV," has a 3.15" dish, .260" deep. The "Camaro" piston, it seems, would offer more compression, but, perhaps, was used only with the iron heads. Is this the difference? If not, why use the "APV" pistons, forced induction?<P>As you can tell, I've been watching this post with some interest....||||reg||5
Z||000055||Will||07-22-2001||10:47 AM||||The iron heads have much larger chambers than the aluminum heads.<BR>The 3400 was used in the Lumina only in aluminum head trim.<BR>The 3.4 was used in the Camaro only in iron head trim.<BR>Thus the Camaro pistons have a shallower dish than the Lumina pistons.<BR>You could use the Lumina pistons with iron heads for forced induction, but the compression ratio comes out to something ridiculously low, in the mid 7's if I remember correctly.||||reg||1
Z||000056||00lE||07-22-2001||02:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>dish than the Lumina pistons.<BR>You could use the Lumina pistons with iron heads for forced induction, but the compression ratio comes out to something ridiculously low, in the mid 7's if I remember correctly.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, the compression woul not end up that low at all.  I belive you are basing this on using the iron heads with a 3.1 MFPI motor which starts out with a measily 8.9:1 compresion ratio.  If you where to use the Lumina 3400 piston(9.5:1 in stock form )with iron heads, the resulting compresion ratio would be very clsoe 7.9:1, which is almost perfect for a high boost turbocharged application.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000057||Patrick's Dad||07-22-2001||08:00 PM||||Actually, I'm working on my parts list for a 3.4/GIII head buildup. What would c/r be for these pistons with the aluminum heads? Will the "Camaro" pistons clear the valves with this head? Will it require unusual octane? etc? ||||reg||1
